<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="mth124-act-linear-functions">

<page>
<paragraphs>
<title>Equations of Lines</title>
<assemblage xml:id="lin-act-review-equations">
<title>Linear Equations</title>
<p>A linear function can be written as <m>y = mx + b</m>, where <m>m</m> is the slope (constant rate of change) and <m>b</m> is the <m>y</m>-intercept (starting value). Alternatively, if you know the slope <m>m</m> and any point <m>(x_1, y_1)</m> on the line, you can use the point-slope form: <m>y - y_1 = m(x - x_1)</m>.</p>
</assemblage>
</paragraphs>

<exercise xml:id="lin-act-two-points" workspace="4.5cm">
<statement>
<p>Find an equation of a line passing through the points <m>(-2,-1)</m> and <m>(1,17)</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>y = 6x + 11</m></p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>
First, find the slope. Then use point-slope form with <m>(-2,-1)</m>.
<md>
<mrow>m \amp= \frac{17-(-1)}{1-(-2)} = \frac{18}{3} = 6</mrow>
<mrow>y - (-1) \amp= 6(x - (-2))</mrow>
<mrow>y + 1 \amp= 6(x + 2)</mrow>
<mrow>y + 1 \amp= 6x + 12</mrow>
<mrow>y \amp= 6x + 11</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="lin-act-slope-intercept" workspace="2cm">
<statement>
<p>Find an equation of a line with slope <m>\displaystyle \frac{1}{3}</m> and <m>y</m>-intercept <m>-5</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>y = \frac{1}{3}x - 5</m></p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>We are given <m>m = \frac{1}{3}</m> and <m>b = -5</m> directly. Substituting these into <m>y = mx + b</m> gives <m>y = \frac{1}{3}x - 5</m>.</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<paragraphs>
<title>Linear Tables</title>
<example xml:id="lin-act-example-tables">
<title>Worked Example</title>
<p>To determine if a table of values represents a linear function, check if the average rate of change between <em>every</em> pair of points is constant. Be careful: the <m>x</m>-values might not increase by a uniform amount, so you must always compute <m>\frac{\Delta y}{\Delta x}</m>.</p>
</example>
</paragraphs>

<exercise xml:id="lin-act-which-linear">
<statement>
<p>Which of the following tables could represent a linear function? Show your rate calculations to justify your answer.</p>
<sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor" right="minor">
<row>
<cell right="major"><m>x</m></cell>
<cell><m>3</m></cell>
<cell><m>4</m></cell>
<cell><m>5</m></cell>
<cell><m>7</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell right="major"><m>f(x)</m></cell>
<cell><m>-2</m></cell>
<cell><m>-5</m></cell>
<cell><m>-8</m></cell>
<cell><m>-11</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor" right="minor">
<row>
<cell right="major"><m>x</m></cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
<cell><m>2</m></cell>
<cell><m>5</m></cell>
<cell><m>7</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell right="major"><m>g(x)</m></cell>
<cell><m>0.25</m></cell>
<cell><m>0.5</m></cell>
<cell><m>1.25</m></cell>
<cell><m>1.75</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<alert>Table for <m>f(x)</m>:</alert>
<md>
<mrow>\frac{-5-(-2)}{4-3} \amp= \frac{-3}{1} = -3</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{-8-(-5)}{5-4} \amp= \frac{-3}{1} = -3</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{-11-(-8)}{7-5} \amp= \frac{-3}{2} = -1.5</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>Because the rate of change drops from <m>-3</m> to <m>-1.5</m>, <m>f(x)</m> is <alert>not</alert> linear. Students often incorrectly assume it is linear by only looking at the pattern in the <m>y</m>-values (which decrease by <m>3</m> each time), failing to notice the jump from <m>x=5</m> to <m>x=7</m> in the input.</p>
<p>
<alert>Table for <m>g(x)</m>:</alert>
<md>
<mrow>\frac{0.5-0.25}{2-1} \amp= \frac{0.25}{1} = 0.25</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{1.25-0.5}{5-2} \amp= \frac{0.75}{3} = 0.25</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{1.75-1.25}{7-5} \amp= \frac{0.5}{2} = 0.25</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>Because the rate of change is consistently <m>0.25</m> between every pair of points, <m>g(x)</m> <alert>could be</alert> linear.</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="lin-act-make-tables" workspace="2.5in">
<statement>
<p>Make two <m>(x,y)</m> tables of your own (with at least <m>4</m> points each). One table should represent a linear pattern, and the other should not.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>Answers will vary. Ensure the linear table has a constant <m>\frac{\Delta y}{\Delta x}</m> ratio, and the non-linear table does not.</p>
</solution>
</exercise>
</page>

<page>
<paragraphs>
<title>Models from Rate and Initial Value</title>
<assemblage xml:id="lin-act-review-sign">
<title>Sign of the Slope</title>
<p>When reading a word problem, pay attention to whether the context describes growth or loss. If a quantity is <alert>increasing</alert>, the slope is <alert>positive</alert>. If a quantity is <alert>decreasing</alert>, the slope is <alert>negative</alert>.</p>
</assemblage>
</paragraphs>

<exercise xml:id="lin-act-phone-plan">
<introduction>
<p>Your phone plan comes with <m>15</m> GB of data, and you use about <m>2.5</m> GB per week.</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="2.5cm">
<statement>
<p>Write a linear equation for your remaining data <m>D</m> (in GB) after <m>w</m> weeks.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>D(w) = -2.5w + 15</m></p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>The starting data is <m>15</m> GB, so <m>b = 15</m>. The data is decreasing, so the rate of change is <m>m = -2.5</m>. The equation is <m>D(w) = -2.5w + 15</m>.</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task workspace="1.5cm">
<statement>
<p>After how many weeks will your data run out?</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>6</m> weeks</p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>
The data runs out when <m>D(w) = 0</m>.
<md>
<mrow>0 \amp= -2.5w + 15</mrow>
<mrow>2.5w \amp= 15</mrow>
<mrow>w \amp= 6</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>The data runs out after <m>6</m> weeks.</p>
</solution>
</task>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="lin-act-car-depreciation">
<introduction>
<p>Suppose <m>5</m> years after buying a new car it is worth $21,250, and when it is <m>20</m> years old it is worth $10,000. Assume the price of the car depreciates linearly.</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="2.5cm">
<statement>
<p>Determine a linear function <m>P(t)</m> that gives the price <m>t</m> years after the car was purchased.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>P(t) = -750t + 25000</m></p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>
Find the slope using <m>(5, 21250)</m> and <m>(20, 10000)</m>, then use point-slope form.
<md>
<mrow>m \amp= \frac{10000-21250}{20-5} = \frac{-11250}{15} = -750</mrow>
<mrow>P - 21250 \amp= -750(t - 5)</mrow>
<mrow>P \amp= -750t + 3750 + 21250</mrow>
<mrow>P(t) \amp= -750t + 25000</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task workspace="2cm">
<statement>
<p>What was the initial value of the car when it was purchased?</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>$25,000</p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>The initial value corresponds to <m>t = 0</m>, which is the vertical intercept. The car was originally purchased for $25,000.</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task workspace="2.5cm">
<statement>
<p>When will the car have no value? Based on this, what is a practical domain for your function?</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>33.33</m> years, <m>[0, 33.33]</m></p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>
The car has no value when <m>P(t) = 0</m>.
<md>
<mrow>0 \amp= -750t + 25000</mrow>
<mrow>750t \amp= 25000</mrow>
<mrow>t \amp= \frac{25000}{750} \approx 33.33</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>The car has no value after about <m>33.33</m> years. The practical domain restricts the model to times before the car's value becomes negative: <m>[0, 33.33]</m>.</p>
</solution>
</task>
</exercise>
</page>

</worksheet>
